The files of this account
29c-sd-tcl-the-development-tools
The list shows every file your VOC points at: what kind it is (dynamic, directory, sequential, a file on another server), about how many records it holds, its modulus and size on disk, how many indices it has, whether it has a trigger, whether its record ids ignore case (any) or do not (exact), whether it has a dictionary, and whether another session has it open. The box under it describes the file under the cursor in words: where it is, how full its groups are, its indices and trigger.
N makes a file (create.file), C clears it (clear.file) and D deletes it (delete.file): each is the verb you could type, run for you after a box that shows the exact command; Cancel is what Enter chooses, and Clear and Delete ask for the file's name, typed. A analyses the file (analyse.file), I lists its indices (list.index) and T its triggers (list.triggers): these only report, and they print straight to the terminal, so the page hands the terminal to the verb and Enter brings the page back, at the same file. A name with a capital, an ampersand or a dollar is listed but not given to a command: the page tells you which verb to type for it.
/ or F11 shows only the names that hold some text; Esc clears it. The gates line shows the edit gates your session holds, asked of the system. Making a new file is not held back by them; clearing or deleting a program file needs bp-edit and a dictionary on its own needs dict-edit. If one is off, the verb's own refusal is what you get and the file is left as it was; the administrator turns a gate on with modify.account.
